      "id": 2,
      "options": {
        "content": "### What the filters published\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `$category` | **${category}** |\n| `$valueMin` | **${valueMin}** |\n| `$valueMax` | **${valueMax}** |\n| `$site` | **${site}** |\n| `$lat` / `$lng` | **${lat}** / **${lng}** |\n\nSet a filter on `category` on the map and the variable follows at once. Add a filter on `value` (a numeric column) and drag its histogram brush: the pair follows ~300 ms after the brush rests — one write per gesture, not one per frame. Consumers use `value BETWEEN '$valueMin' AND '$valueMax'`; both variables go blank when the filter is removed. Both directions work: type bounds into the boxes above and the map's filter follows.\n\n`$site` is the *click* mapping: click a point on the map and the variable takes its `site` — `WHERE site = '$site'` on any other panel. Click empty map to clear it. One way only: typing in the box does not move the map.\n\n`$lat`/`$lng` is the *coordinates* mapping: any click also pins the place clicked and the pair takes it, for a spatial query on another panel — `ST_DWithin(geom, ST_MakePoint($lng, $lat)::geography, $radius)` with `$radius` a plain dashboard variable. Clicking again unpins on the map but the pair keeps the last spot; the next pin moves it.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "title": "Published range",
      "type": "text"
    }
